### Summary of Key Insights
The evidence shows that during the October 22, 2020 presidential debate, Donald Trump did not state that "$15 an hour is too much for essential workers." Instead, Trump expressed opposition to a uniform federal minimum wage mandate of $15 an hour, arguing that it would be "ruinous" for some regions and would force small businesses to lay off employees. He advocated that the minimum wage should be a "state option" and expressed openness to some states adopting a $15 wage. His arguments were focused on the general economic impact on small businesses and employment, rather than specifically targeting essential workers.

### Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that Donald Trump said "$15 an hour is too much for essential workers" is inaccurate. Trump's actual statements focused on his opposition to a federal mandate due to its potential negative impact on small businesses, while suggesting that a $15 minimum wage could be appropriate as a state-level decision. He did not make any statements targeting essential workers or claiming they do not deserve this wage. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
During the October 22, 2020 presidential debate, Donald Trump did not state that a $15 hourly wage is too much for essential workers. According to [NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/wages-was-most-searched-issue-google-during-final-trump-biden-n1244547), Trump opposed a uniform federal minimum wage mandate, arguing instead that it should be a "state option" because "every state is different." He warned that a federal mandate would be "ruinous" and, as detailed by [Vox](https://www.vox.com/2020/10/22/21529733/donald-trump-minimum-wage-debate), would force small businesses to fire many of their employees. Furthermore, [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/view/articles/2020-10-23/a-15-minimum-wage-would-hurt-biden-s-economy) noted that Trump actually expressed openness to a $15-per-hour wage on a state-by-state basis. His arguments focused entirely on the general economic impact on small businesses and employment rather than targeting essential workers.